<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Talas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/751">751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Talas River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Talas was fought in 751 near the Talas River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, where a Tang army was defeated by an Abbasid-allied force. It is often cited as a marker of the limit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> expansion westward. Tradition also links the battle to the spread of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">paper-making</a> to the Islamic world through captured artisans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas.jpg" alt="Battle of Talas" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Talas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751, where a Tang army under Gao Xianzhi was defeated by Abbasid forces after the defection of its Karluk allies, is often cited as marking the limit of Tang westward expansion, and is traditionally linked with the transmission of paper-making westward through captured…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Bayanchur Khan the khaganate brought the Sekiz Oghuz, Kyrgyz, Karluks, Türgesh, Toquz Tatars and Chiks under its rule, and after the Tang defeat by the Arabs at the <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751 the Uyghurs emerged as the dominant power of Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turks first met Islam at the <b>Battle of Talas</b> and converted largely through Sufism; in the 14th century Özbeg Khan of the Golden Horde turned to Islam, and the Turkic and Mongolic peoples of Central Asia largely converted.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven west by the Uyghur Khaganate, they moved to the Zhetysu region, and in 751 their defection to the Abbasid side helped decide the <b>Battle of Talas</b> against Tang China.</blockquote><a href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a></li><li><blockquote>In 750 the Tang general Gao Xianzhi executed the ruler of Chach, whose son sought help from the Abbasids; the resulting <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751 ended Chinese influence west of the Tian Shan.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chach">Principality of Chach</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferghana was contested between the Umayyads, the Turgesh Khaganate and Tang China until after the <b>Battle of Talas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Principality of Ferghana</a></li></ul></section>
